  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 201 AM MDT Tue Sep 8 2026\n\nToday through Fri...A weak trough moving through western MT \nwill bring only a slight chance threat of showers and \nthunderstorms to the upper Snake River highlands. By this \nevening, the trough is well east of the forecast area. West \nnorthwest flow continues Wed and shifts during the day Thu to\nmore westerly. As the next trough approaches from western\nCanada, the gradient tightens on Thu and has the potential to \nreach just below Wind Advisory criteria on both Thu and Fri\nafternoons. \n\n500mb heights rise the next two days, allowing some significant\nwarming. The trough approaching for Fri should spread some\nclouds and bring in cooler air aloft, which should eventually\nbring cooler temperatures at the surface, likely on Fri. \n\nFri night through Mon...A low breaks off the trough, perhaps as\nthis scenario is not featured in every solution cluster. This\nshifts the airflow to a southerly component that draws up\nsubtropical moisture, as well as bringing some available\nmoisture itself. This brings a risk of showers and thunderstorms\nto the forecast area. First, for the southern and eastern\nhighlands, then on Sun, as the closed low moves through central\nand northern Idaho, to the centeral Idaho mountains. None of it\nappears very heavy, but it should be unstable enough to trigger\nthunderstorms. Strong wind possible Sat night/Sun morning as the\nlow pushes through the northern half of the forecast area.\nTemperatures should sink to near to below normal for this\nperiod, with the coldest temperatures just after trough passage\non Sun.",
